A||||5||DISenchanted||Tires, again...||||1||
Z||000000||DISenchanted||02-03-2000||04:44 PM||||I did a search and came up empty.  What is the widest tire that i can fit on the rear of an 88gt.  I noticed that on the 88gt, the rear rims are different.  I want to squeze either 235 or 245's in there.  If I do go with the larger tires, how would I recaliberate my speedometer?  Thanks,<P>DISenchanted||||reg||1
Z||000001||Monkeyman||02-03-2000||04:56 PM||||If you get some really wide wheels and hang about 3 feet outside the fenderwells, you could put some huge tires on your Fiero.||||reg||1
Z||000002||86SE||02-03-2000||09:01 PM||||I should make my Fiero a duele and put chains on the rear tires!!||||reg||10
Z||000003||Phaeton||02-03-2000||11:20 PM||||Unless suspention mods are being done also it's best to stay with stock location and diameter.  After about 245 the centerline starts creeping out, helping to fix the understeer problem while creating tire wear and torque steer.  Depending on usage this may or may not be noticable.||||reg||1
Z||000004||fiero56||02-04-2000||01:50 PM||||torque steer=front wheel drive<BR>no torque steer=rear wheel drive||||reg||1
Z||000005||Eric||02-04-2000||02:23 PM||||A good comprimise would be 225/60/15 tires on the back with the stock 205s on front.  This combination is what I have on my 88GT and it looks better.  The sidewall is a little higher (puts my speedo 3mph off at 60).  You could try 245/50/15s, but you will have too much sidewall deflection for the tires to really do their job.  If you're thinking of uprgrading your rims to 17s, I think the ideal size would be 225/45/17s with 7.5 or 8 inch wheels on front and 255/40/17s on 8 or 9 inch wheels for the back.||||reg||1
